#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Usage: me doc
# Summary: Me-family reference: layout, hidden API, how to add one

# me API
case "${1:-}" in
    --summary) echo "Me-family reference: layout, hidden API, how to add one"; exit 0 ;;
    --complete|__complete)
        printf '%s\n' --help -h
        exit 0 ;;
    -h|--help)
        echo "Usage: me doc"
        echo ""
        echo "  Prints the layout, the hidden API, both templates, the checklist for"
        echo "  adding a subcommand, and where each piece lives on this machine."
        echo "  Takes no arguments."
        echo ""
        echo "Script: ${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
        exit 0 ;;
    "") ;;
    *)
        echo "me doc: takes no arguments (got '$1')." >&2
        echo "Usage: me doc" >&2
        exit 2 ;;
esac

cat <<'EOF'
# The `me` command family — this machine's layout

`me` is a 151-line dispatcher in `~/script`. `me <cmd>` looks for an
executable named `me-<cmd>` on `PATH`, then in the dispatcher's own
directory (`$DIR`). No arguments means `help`.

Everything below describes the tree as it is on this machine, not a
generic scaffold. For the generic guide, see the `me-subcmd` skill.

## Layouts

| Layout | Structure | How it is found |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Flat** (preferred) | `me`, `me-<cmd>` as separate executables | `command -v me-<cmd>`, anywhere on `PATH` |
| **Flat+Dir** | `me-<cmd>/_main_` plus other files | `me-<cmd>/_main_` on `PATH` or beside the dispatcher |
| **Dir** (legacy) | `me`, `cmd/<subcmd>` | scan of `cmd/` next to the dispatcher |

Real examples on this machine:

- `me-ai` lives in `~/script`; its leaf `me-ai-log` lives in
  `~/dotwiki/tool`. Both directories are on `PATH`, so the pair is Flat
  even though the files sit apart. `me-ai-update` is in the same tool
  directory.
- `me-tool-trace/` in `~/script` is Flat+Dir: `_main_` is a bash wrapper
  carrying the me API, and `trace.py` is the implementation. It sits beside
  `me-tool` so `me tool trace` has exactly one spelling.
- `me-tool` is a dispatcher over two places: `~/dotwiki/tool/me-<name>` and
  `$DIR/me-tool-<name>` beside itself. It subtracts a hardcoded `skip_cmds`
  list so commands reachable through another parent (`ai-log`, `fgt-*`,
  `update-*`, …) do not appear twice. **Lookup and listing must agree** —
  when only the lookup knew about `$DIR`, a leaf resolved but never appeared
  in `me tool -h`. `me-tool-tag` and `me-tool-sshfs` are plain leaves
  there; they used to be `me tag` and `me sshfs`, which is why they sit
  beside their dispatcher.
- `me-tmux` is a dispatcher over `me-tmux-*` beside it in `~/script`. The
  live-session actions (`ctl`, `mode`, `share`, `unshare`, `update`,
  `kill`, `select`, `zoom`, `cmd`) all execute `me-tmux-ctl`: the seven
  action names are **symlinks** to it and the action defaults from
  `basename "$0"`. Symlinks rather than an alias table in the dispatcher
  because nested completion never goes through the parent — the shell
  adapter calls `me __complete "tmux-mode"`, `me` resolves that with
  `command -v me-tmux-mode`, and only a real `PATH` entry completes.
  `me-tmux-workspace` is a separate leaf.
- `me-update-2fa` is reached both as `me update 2fa` and, from inside
  `me update repo`, as a helper: the parent asks it whether a 2FA code is
  needed before the first pull. A leaf does not have to be a leaf only.
- **Leaf placement decides who can reach it.** `me-tool` scans
  `~/dotwiki/tool` for `me-<name>`, so any leaf left there is also
  reachable as `me tool <name>` whatever parent it was written for. Putting
  `me-fgt-conf` next to `me-fgt` in `~/script` keeps it reachable only as
  `me fgt conf`. Move the file, not the code, when you want one spelling.
- `me-git` + `me-git-add` and `me-doc` are Flat: one executable each,
  both in `~/script`.

Prefer Flat. Reach for Flat+Dir only when a subcommand needs more than
one file (a Python module, several scripts); never add new Dir layouts.

## The tree as it stands

```
me
├── doc         me-family reference (this document)
├── ai          (log, update)
├── clang       clang/lint helpers
├── fgt         (addr2line, build, conf, craddr, eco, pcap)
├── git         (add)
├── tmux        (ctl, mode, share, unshare, update, kill, select, zoom, cmd, workspace)
├── tool        (conf-putty, sshfs, tag, trace)
└── update      (2fa, dot, nvim, repo, share, wiki)
```

Files: `~/script/me-*` for most leaves and parents, `~/dotwiki/tool/me-*`
for the leaves that were always tool-directory resident. `me`'s list above
is hardcoded and must be updated by hand; `me <parent> -h` for any parent
derives its own table from the files on disk.

## Two dispatchers that behave differently

`me` (top level) and `me-tool` are the two that do not simply scan one
directory, and both have tripped us:

| | `me` | `me-tool` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Lists by | hardcoded table | scanning |
| Avoids child shadowing by | `grep -v '-'` in `__commands` | `skip_cmds` |
| Extra detail | — | scans **two** dirs (see below) |

`me-tool` resolves `me tool <name>` in `~/dotwiki/tool/me-<name>` **and**
in `$DIR/me-tool-<name>` beside itself. Because `~/dotwiki/tool` is the
same directory most leaves live in, any leaf there is reachable as
`me tool <name>` no matter which parent it was written for — that is why
`ai-log` and friends are in `skip_cmds`. A leaf meant to be reachable only
through its real parent must live in `~/script`.

## The hidden API

The dispatcher answers these tokens; they exist for the shell, not for
people:

| Token | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `__commands` | subcommand names, one per line, sorted |
| `__complete <subcmd>` | option list for `<subcmd>` (newline-separated) |
| `__help <subcmd>` | the subcommand's one-line `--summary` |
| `completion bash\|zsh` | print the completion adapter |

Every leaf answers:

| Token | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--summary` | one-line description, exit 0 |
| `--complete` / `__complete` | newline-separated options, exit 0 |
| `-h` / `--help` | full usage, exit 0 |

`--complete` and `__complete` are aliases on the leaf; the dispatcher
always calls `__complete`. Output must be newline-separated — zsh's
`${(@f)...}` splits on newlines and would treat a space-separated list
as one candidate.

A dispatcher's `help` loops its leaves, runs `--summary` on each, and
pads the name to 15 columns:

```bash
for sub in $(list_subcmds); do
    p="$(find_subcmd "$sub")"
    s="$("$p" --summary 2>/dev/null || true)"
    printf "  %-15s %s\n" "$sub" "$s"
done
```

## A leaf, end to end

This is the whole wrapper from `me-ai-update`: a usage comment pair, the
me-API `case`, then the real body. Twenty lines, no framework.

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Usage: me ai update [all|install|clean|login] [-n]
# Summary: Update the fos-ai Claude Code install and re-login

# me API
case "${1:-}" in
    --summary) echo "Update the fos-ai Claude Code install and re-login"; exit 0 ;;
    --complete|__complete)
        printf '%s\n' --help -h -n --dryrun
        printf '%s\n' all install clean login
        exit 0 ;;
    -h|--help)
        echo "Usage: me ai update [all|install|clean|login] [-n]"
        echo "  -n, --dryrun  Print the commands without executing them"
        echo ""
        echo "Script: ${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
        exit 0 ;;
esac

# implementation starts here...
```

Rules that matter:

- `printf '%s\n'`, never `echo`, for the option list — `echo -n`
  prints nothing.
- `-h` ends with `Script: ${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`.
- The `# Usage:` and `# Summary:` pair at the top and the `--summary`
  string must agree.

## A dispatcher, end to end

`me-ai` (94 lines) is the reference shape. It defines two functions and
a `case`:

1. `find_ai_subcmd <cmd>` — try `$DIR/<SELF>-<cmd>` as a directory with
   `_main_`, then as an executable, then the same two forms in each
   `PATH` entry.
2. `list_ai_subcmds` — scan every `PATH` entry once (`seen_dirs`),
   print `me-ai-*` executables and `_main_` directories, dedupe with
   `sort -u`.
3. `case "$cmd"` handling `--summary`, `__commands`, `__complete`,
   `__help`, `help|-h|--help`, then a trailing `shift` + `exec "$p" "$@"`.

To make a new dispatcher, copy `~/script/me-ai` and rename three things:
`find_ai_subcmd`, `list_ai_subcmds`, and the `--summary` text. Nothing
else changes.

```bash
sed -e 's/find_ai_subcmd/find_git_subcmd/g' \
    -e 's/list_ai_subcmds/list_git_subcmds/g' \
    -e 's/"AI subcommands"/"Git helpers"/' \
    ~/script/me-ai > ~/script/me-git
chmod 755 ~/script/me-git
```

Two differences from `me` itself, which is older:

- `me`'s own help output is a hardcoded list (lines 131-141 of
  `~/script/me`). It does **not** auto-discover; a new top-level command
  stays invisible and incomplete until you add a line there.
- `me` filters hyphenated names out of `__commands`
  (`grep -v '-'`) so `me-ai-log` does not show as a top-level command.
  `me-ai`/`me-git`/`me-tool` do not filter — they are parents already.

## Adding one

1. Pick the layout — Flat unless the command needs several files.
2. Create `~/script/me-<name>`, mode 755.
3. Implement the API tokens: `--summary`, `--complete`/`__complete`
   (`printf '%s\n' --help -h ...`), `-h`/`--help`.
4. Test the leaf directly:
   `me-<name> --summary`, `me-<name> -h`, `me-<name> __complete ""`.
5. Add a line to `me`'s hardcoded help list in `~/script/me` — otherwise
   `me -h` and `me __commands` never mention it.
6. Add a row to `~/script/me_readme.md` (the deploy notes).
7. Check the dispatcher view: `me -h`, `me __complete ""`,
   `me help <name>`, and completion.

For a family under a new parent (like `me git`), also add the parent's
line to `me`'s list, and give the parent a `--summary` of its own so
`me __help <parent>` is not blank.

## Completion and debugging

Bash:

```bash
source <(me completion bash)
```

Zsh:

```bash
me completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_me
autoload -Uz compinit && compinit
```

When completion misbehaves:

```bash
me __complete ""            # top-level candidates
me __complete git           # options for one subcommand
command -v me-git-add       # is the leaf actually on PATH?
```

Two gotchas recorded from real failures:

- `echo -n` in a `--complete` handler prints nothing. Use `printf`.
- A child subcommand must not shadow its parent. `me-ai-log` must not
  be listed as top-level `ai-log`, which is why `me` filters hyphens
  and `me-tool` keeps a `skip_cmds` list.

## Where things live

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `~/script/me` | the dispatcher; hardcoded help list at the bottom |
| `~/script/me-lib.sh` | `me_init` / `die` / `cleanup`; used by `me-tool-tag`, `me-tmux-ctl`, `me-update-*` |
| `~/script/me-<name>` | leaf or parent subcommands |
| `~/script/me-tool-<name>` | `me tool` leaves beside the dispatcher: `tag`, `sshfs` (plain leaves) |
| `~/script/me-tmux-<name>` | `me tmux` leaves beside the dispatcher: `ctl`, `workspace`; the action names are symlinks to `me-tmux-ctl` |
| `~/dotwiki/tool/` | second `PATH` directory: `me-ai-log`, `me-update-2fa`, `me-update-share`, `me-conf-putty`, `me-fgt-*` |
| `~/script/me-fgt-*` | `me fgt` leaves beside their dispatcher: `conf`, `build` |
| `~/script/me-tool-trace/` | `me tool trace`, Flat+Dir; `trace.py` reads `ME_TRACE_CONFIG_DIR` (default `~/dotwiki/config`) |
| `~/dotwiki/config/trace.d/` | trace targets (`wad`, `wad-64`, `dns`, …), one dir each with `conf.ini` |
| `~/dotwiki/_removed/` | retired leaves, kept out of `PATH` so no dispatcher lists them |
| `~/script/me_readme.md` | deploy notes: PATH, completions, the decision table |
| `~/.claude-share/skills/me-subcmd/` | the generic scaffolding guide and templates |

### Every file, by dispatcher

`~/script` is on `PATH`; `~/dotwiki/tool` is on `PATH` too. A leaf listed
under a parent is reachable as `me <parent> <leaf>` from its own directory,
and additionally as `me tool <leaf>` if it sits in `~/dotwiki/tool`.

| Command | File |
| --- | --- |
| `me doc` | `~/script/me-doc` |
| `me ai log` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-ai-log` |
| `me ai update` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-ai-update` |
| `me clang` | `~/script/me-clang` |
| `me fgt addr2line` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-fgt-addr2line` |
| `me fgt build` | `~/script/me-fgt-build` |
| `me fgt conf` | `~/script/me-fgt-conf` |
| `me fgt craddr` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-fgt-craddr` |
| `me fgt eco` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-fgt-eco` |
| `me fgt pcap` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-fgt-pcap` |
| `me git add` | `~/script/me-git-add` |
| `me tool conf-putty` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-conf-putty` |
| `me tool sshfs` | `~/script/me-tool-sshfs` |
| `me tool tag` | `~/script/me-tool-tag` |
| `me tool trace` | `~/script/me-tool-trace/_main_` (+ `trace.py`) |
| `me tmux ctl` | `~/script/me-tmux-ctl` (also `mode`, `share`, `unshare`, `update`, `kill`, `select`, `zoom`, `cmd` via symlinks) |
| `me tmux workspace` | `~/script/me-tmux-workspace` |
| `me update 2fa` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-update-2fa` |
| `me update dot` | `~/script/me-update-dot` |
| `me update nvim` | `~/script/me-update-nvim` |
| `me update repo` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-update-repo` |
| `me update share` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-update-share` |
| `me update wiki` | `~/dotwiki/tool/me-update-wiki` |

Retired, kept in `~/dotwiki/_removed/` (not on `PATH`): `me-config`,
`me-config-gitvan`, `me-sync`. Their replacements are `me tool …`,
`me update 2fa tunnel`, and `me update share`.

Which answers what:

- **`me doc`** — this machine's actual tree. Read it first.
- **the `me-subcmd` skill** — the generic guide: layouts, templates,
  refactoring from `cmd/` to flat.
- **`me_readme.md`** — install and deploy steps.
EOF
